6. DYNAMIC CSV SCHEMA MAPPING

Do not assume that MediaCore CSV exports will always have exactly the same headers or column ordering.

The system must inspect the actual CSV headers.

Implement a schema mapping layer.

Important business fields include:

- OP Name.
- TP Name.
- OP ANI.
- TP ANI.
- Country.
- State.
- Message Status.
- Message Reason.
- Applicant.
- Direction.
- Message Timestamp.
- Source.
- Destination.
- Message ID.
- Transaction ID.
- Route.
- Any additional available MediaCore fields.

These are logical field names, not guaranteed CSV headers.

Create a configurable mapping interface.

For example:

CSV COLUMN                  SYSTEM FIELD

op_name                     OP_NAME
operator                    OP_NAME
origin_operator             OP_NAME

tp_name                     TP_NAME
provider                    TP_NAME
termination_provider        TP_NAME

Allow multiple CSV headers to map to the same logical field.

If an essential field is unavailable, notify the user.

If an optional field is missing, continue processing and disable only the analytical features that depend on that field.

Preserve unknown columns where practical, subject to configurable storage and schema rules.

Provide a preview of the first 20–50 rows before confirming an unfamiliar schema.

Save reusable schema mapping profiles.

Do not silently guess ambiguous column mappings.

